Based on PropInspect's North Carolina guide, here's what NC law allows a landlord to take from a deposit.

**What a landlord can deduct (N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-51)**
Only these items:
- Unpaid rent
- Damage beyond normal wear and tear
- Unpaid utility charges or late fees owed under the lease
- Re-rental costs if you broke the lease
- Removal and storage costs after an eviction
- Court costs

Normal wear, like faded paint or carpet worn from ordinary use, can't be deducted.

**How this applies to your cleaning charge**
"Cleaning" isn't on the list by itself. To keep money from your deposit for it, the landlord would have to fit it into one of the categories above, most likely damage beyond normal wear and tear. The guide doesn't say how a cleaning fee you already paid under the lease affects a later cleaning deduction, so I can't tell you for sure whether the extra charge is allowed. These questions can help:
- What does your lease say the $260 fee covers? If it covers move-out cleaning, an extra cleaning charge looks like charging you twice for the same thing.
- Is the extra charge for actual damage, or just routine turnover cleaning?

**Rules that can help you**
- **30-day deadline:** Within 30 days after you move out and return possession, the landlord must give you an itemized list of damages plus the rest of your deposit. If they can't total the damages yet, they owe you an interim accounting at 30 days and a final one within 60. Ask them to itemize the extra cleaning charge.
- **Trust account or bond:** The deposit has to be kept in a trust account at a licensed, federally insured NC bank, or covered by a bond. They must tell you where it's held within 30 days of the lease starting.
- **Penalty:** If the landlord willfully breaks the deposit, bond or notice rules, they lose the right to keep any of the deposit, even for real damage. The law doesn't add double damages or attorney's fees.

**If you can't resolve it**
Deposit disputes go to small claims court before a magistrate, for claims up to $10,000. Bring:
- Your lease, including the cleaning-fee clause and your receipt for the $260
- The landlord's itemized statement, or proof that you never got one
- The trust account or bond notice, if you got one
- Dated photos from move-in and move-out

**Source:** PropInspect, North Carolina Security Deposit Laws, https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/north-carolina (last reviewed September 18, 2026). It's based on the Tenant Security Deposit Act, N.C. Gen. Stat. §§ 42-50 to 42-56 ([official text](https://www.ncleg.gov/enactedlegislation/statutes/html/byarticle/chapter_42/article_6.html)).

*This is general information, not legal advice. For your specific dispute, talk to a North Carolina landlord-tenant attorney or a local tenant rights group.*